From 5f68606ab363095b5c2d3d758a8459052349c293 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:22:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: what is known of trims for a display between
 two targets

The header of the trim pass said that no public document defines how to take
a level 2 block to a display whose peak falls between the targets of two
blocks. It now says what the public documents do say, and what was measured.

SMPTE ST 2094-1 leaves the use and selection of metadata sets out of the
standard; ST 2094-10 and ETSI TS 103 572 say nothing of a display between
targets. Dolby's UHD Blu-ray authoring guide and its grading guide say that
its display management and its grading tool interpolate trims, without
saying how. A linear blend of the five codes in PQ, as other builds make it,
was measured against the colourist's own blocks of two streams, each block of
a target made again from its neighbours: worse than no trim on the neutral
ramp in 11 of 11 scenes (a blended offset crushes the shadows to black), and
the colourist's blocks in between are no blend of their neighbours. The
nearest block was the same as none. A display without a block of its own peak
keeps getting no trim; nothing in the code changes.
---
 tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_trims.h | 24 ++++++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_trims.h b/tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_trims.h
index 585e0a5..1eb1c80 100644
--- a/tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_trims.h
+++ b/tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_trims.h
@@ -37,16 +37,20 @@
  * quoting SMPTE EG 432-1; here from the primaries of the display it is mapped to.)
  *
  * Which block: the standard defines no way to take a trim pass to a display that is not the one it
- * was
- * made for (ST 2094-1 only says that a receiver can select the sets that fit the display best), so
- * a
- * pass is used only for the display it was made for: the block whose target_max_PQ is the display's
- * peak in 12-bit PQ, to within one code (what the rounding of a peak in cd/m2 to a code is). With
- * no
- * such block the picture is mapped as it was before, bit for bit; no interpolation between the
- * targets
- * of two blocks, which no public document defines. ms_weight is reserved in TS 103 572 (a decoder
- * ignores it; the streams at hand carry 2048 there, not the -1 that its text says).
+ * was made for (ST 2094-1 only says that a receiver can select the sets that fit the display best,
+ * and that "operational details about use and selection of metadata sets are not part of this
+ * document"), so a pass is used only for the display it was made for: the block whose target_max_PQ
+ * is the display's peak in 12-bit PQ, to within one code (what the rounding of a peak in cd/m2 to a
+ * code is). With no such block the picture is mapped as it was before, bit for bit. Dolby documents
+ * that its own display management and grading tools interpolate trims for targets in between (its
+ * UHD Blu-ray authoring guide, and the "Interpolate" function of its grading guide), but does not
+ * publish how; no standard defines it. A linear blend of the five codes in PQ, as other builds make
+ * it, was measured against the colourist's own blocks of two streams (each block of a target left
+ * out and made from its neighbours): worse than no trim on the neutral ramp in 11 of 11 scenes, by up
+ * to 588 codes of 12-bit PQ (a blended offset crushes the shadows to black), and the colourist's
+ * in-between blocks are no blend of their neighbours; the nearest block is the same as none there.
+ * ms_weight is reserved in TS 103 572 (a decoder ignores it; the streams at hand carry 2048 there,
+ * not the -1 that its text says).
  *
  * Header-only, no libplacebo or FFmpeg, so that the host tests include it; the metadata are read in
  * dvbridge_placebo.c (dvbridge_trim_select()), the pass is drawn in dvbridge_render.c.
